#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578877 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t,\\hspace{1em}y={\\operatorname{csc}}^{3}t,\\hspace{2em}0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3{csc}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-3{csc}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}{2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t} \\\\  & =-3{csc}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡t{cot}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{3}⁡t}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡t}{{sin}^{5}⁡t}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t{cot}^{4}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){cot}^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\, \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581711 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,0,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,0,-2)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,0,-2).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,0,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =4x\\text{ }i+4y\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,-2) & =4(1)\\,i+4(0)\\,j-2(-2)\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $4\\,i+4\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582344 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{1}{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582346 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2\\pi x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2\\pi  \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2\\pi x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))\\cdot 2\\pi \\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0))+\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)) \\\\  & =-2(-1-0)+\\frac{2}{\\pi }(0-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582361 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}4x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{4x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into  the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x. \\\\  & =x{e}^{4x}-\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}4x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/2}-{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/2}-{\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4(1/2)}-0\\cdot {e}^{4\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{4(1/2)}-{e}^{4\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}-0]-\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582385 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =2(0-0)+\\frac{2}{3}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582409 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{5x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{5x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{5x}}{5}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{5x}}{5}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{e}^{5x}}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{5x}}{5}-\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583011 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\pi )-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\pi )\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-1$ and then subtracting $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\pi )\\le 1 \\\\  & 1\\ge -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\pi )\\ge -1 \\\\  & -1\\ge \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\pi )-2\\ge -3 \\\\  & -3\\le f(x)\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-3,-1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583037 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=-2\\pi ,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|-2\\pi |=2\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583038 :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le 1 \\\\  & -2\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le 2 \\\\  & -5\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3\\le -1 \\\\  & -5\\le f(x)\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-5,-1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583051 :question/problem "Find the range of the function $y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\le 1 \\\\  & -6\\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\le 6 \\\\  & -6\\le y\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [-6,6].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583083 :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4x}{7}-{32}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{4}{7}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{360}^{∘}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)}={630}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583087 :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=3.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583091 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $-13$ and a maximum value of $-1.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-13+(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{2} \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186584343 :question/problem "![](images/08/08d900690b841d132b3451ca7f8e5013ee1e9a5383c541c926f9e921639d0e5f.png)\n\nLet $C(0,2)$ and $D(4,-1).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{CD}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=0i+2j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=4i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} \\\\  & =d-c \\\\  & =(4i-j)-(2j) \\\\  & =4i-3j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{CD}| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186584366 :question/problem "![](images/89/8927e58849984fbb42a7a3856e3b3509b9729b65031e066d29c6f94747ee5b6b.png)\n\nPoints $C$ and $D$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{CD}|.$" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain $C(-3,4)$ and $D(2,3).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=-3i+4j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} \\\\  & =d-c \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-(-3i+4j) \\\\  & =5i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{CD}| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186584387 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-3,5⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585050 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=-1$ gives a parabola\n\nII. $c=0$ gives the $y$-axis, the $x$-axis and the line $y=x$\n\nIII. $c=1$ gives the line $y=2x$" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y & =c \\\\ xy(y-x) & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is $xy(y-x)=-1,$ which is not a parabola. So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is $xy(y-x)=0,$ which gives the $y$-axis, the $x$-axis and the line $y=x.$ So, II is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is $xy(y-x)=1,$ which is not the line $y=2x.$ So, III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585088 :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[6]{x}$ equals{{field-1}} $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[6]{x}={x}^{1/6}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{6}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/6+1}}{(\\frac{1}{6}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/6}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/6})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{6}{x}^{7/6-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/6}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586552 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,{3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({3}^{x})}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+{3}^{x}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+{3}^{x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-{3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586559 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{y}}{2x}+2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,1,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1) & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{1}}{2(1)}+2(1) \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586560 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\sqrt{x})^{′}\\cdot {e}^{x}+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}{e}^{x}+\\sqrt{x}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586574 :question/problem "![](images/72/721f8231c38ba5937b4e2f80233f83ecb52605388897291d14968064f214a4c4.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest tenth, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/2b/2bdf5043e8bd25884eeda042c276fad2e5fa267e1f9f19d9271564576965d09a.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 10\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{110}^{∘} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{110}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈28.190\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 13\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{58}^{∘} \\\\  & =97.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{58}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈82.684\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =28.190\\,7+82.684\\,7 \\\\  & =110.875\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest tenth, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $110.9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186587304 :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ equals{{field-1}} $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[4]{x}={x}^{1/4}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/4}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590066 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5{e}^{3t},\\,y=6{t}^{2}-9,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=15{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=15{e}^{3\\cdot 0}=15\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590810 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2yz+xyz$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,2,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2yz+xyz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2yz+xyz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-2yz+xyz)\\,k \\\\  & =(2x+yz)\\,i+(-2z+xz)\\,j+(-2y+xy)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,2,-1) & =(2(1)+(2)(-1))\\,i+(-2(-1)+(1)(-1))\\,j+(-2(2)+(1)(2))\\,k \\\\  & =j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\,j-2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,2,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592241 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-v & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=4$, what is the value of $v?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-v & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-v\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-3v-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=4$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =4 \\\\ -3v-20 & =4 \\\\ -3v & =4+20 \\\\ -3v & =24 \\\\ v & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592242 :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =7\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =28-10 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592254 :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1-4\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-4\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592292 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =6\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592302 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x+({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593222 :question/problem "Let  $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨6,2,7⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨-3,-1,3⟩.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{u}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{v} & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {v}_{z} \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-1)+(7)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-18-2+21 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593223 :question/problem "$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x(\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+1)=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x(\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+1) & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}}+\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}x}}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & =({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x(\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+1)=1+\\operatorname{cos}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593227 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}=\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}-\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}={\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}x-1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593272 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{p}=⟨5,-2,0,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,3,10,-5⟩$, then $\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}+{p}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}+{p}_{3}\\cdot {q}_{3}+{p}_{4}\\cdot {q}_{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 10+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5-6+0-15 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593287 :question/problem "What is the value of $x$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $a⊥b$ ($a$ and $b$ are perpendicular) if and only if $a\\cdot b=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 4+5\\cdot x \\\\  & =-6x-4+5x \\\\  & =-x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593288 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $\\mathbf{u}⊥\\mathbf{v}$, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $u⊥v$ ($a$ and $b$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =a\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =a-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a-8 & =0 \\\\ a & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593289 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,0,-1,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨2,1,1,-3⟩$, then $\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{w}=$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot w & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{v}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{v}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{v}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2+0-1-9 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593299 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,y\\ne {x}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne {x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne$ sign, the curve is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $y={x}^{2}$ and shade *above* and *below* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/e6/e6fff00937aa9a20c6e8c1e83dd65c483ae6b403b55b4ac3054d4032249ffedb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594665 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[({t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=({t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)={t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{2}+3)^{′}=2t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[({t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =4t({t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
